    {
      "id": "1bXNUHOpVWeCg_j9STMGp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1267.9121903646605,
      "y": 1394.5881142134774,
      "width": 1003.5391235351562,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6T",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2105007319,
      "version": 564,
      "versionNonce": 2094231735,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1752073580157,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Approach 1  {Brute Force}\n\nThis approach generates all possible pairs which satisfies\nthe mentioned condition in the question by iterating over and over again .\nHas a time complexity of O(n*3) and uniqueness of the triplets can be maintained by sorting then and \nadding them to a set and at the end returning all the unique values in the set \n\n\nfor(int i=0; i<n ;i++){\n    for(int j=i+1;j<n ; j++){\n        for( int k=j+1 ; k<n ;k++){\n        }\n    }\n}",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Approach 1  {Brute Force}\n\nThis approach generates all possible pairs which satisfies\nthe mentioned condition in the question by iterating over and over again .\nHas a time complexity of O(n*3) and uniqueness of the triplets can be maintained by sorting then and \nadding them to a set and at the end returning all the unique values in the set \n\n\nfor(int i=0; i<n ;i++){\n    for(int j=i+1;j<n ; j++){\n        for( int k=j+1 ; k<n ;k++){\n        }\n    }\n}",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "RukoZPT-gfQgXqGdX-QKn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -188.48801425584827,
      "y": 1410.4567902161873,
      "width": 1163.3592529296875,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 299741527,
      "version": 974,
      "versionNonce": 1677814873,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1752074176204,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Approach 2 \n\n\nSemi-optimized Solution is to try and fix O(n*3) => 0(n*2) is some way\nwe can use the same logic as two sum and try to fix a element so the rest two elements \nthat need to be searched are just same as finding two sum form rest of the array \nint [k] = -( int[i]+int[j] );\n\nIn this approach we initialize two pointers int i=0 & int j=i+1;\nnow for all the possible pairs of i and j we take them and sort them to avoid having repetitive pairs\nand them add them inside of a Hashtable to maintain the uniqueness of the answers \nBy doing this we can get rid of inner loop\n\nNote that to maintain the uniqueness of the answers we do have to keep sorting all the valid pairs which are possible \n\nfor(int i=0;i<n;i++){\n    for(int j=i+1;j<n;j++){\n          //Implementing the hashtable\n    \n        }\n    \n}",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Approach 2 \n\n\nSemi-optimized Solution is to try and fix O(n*3) => 0(n*2) is some way\nwe can use the same logic as two sum and try to fix a element so the rest two elements \nthat need to be searched are just same as finding two sum form rest of the array \nint [k] = -( int[i]+int[j] );\n\nIn this approach we initialize two pointers int i=0 & int j=i+1;\nnow for all the possible pairs of i and j we take them and sort them to avoid having repetitive pairs\nand them add them inside of a Hashtable to maintain the uniqueness of the answers \nBy doing this we can get rid of inner loop\n\nNote that to maintain the uniqueness of the answers we do have to keep sorting all the valid pairs which are possible \n\nfor(int i=0;i<n;i++){\n    for(int j=i+1;j<n;j++){\n          //Implementing the hashtable\n    \n        }\n    \n}",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
